### Running the program
To run the code, we will first need to compile it. We can use the `gcc` command for this:
```
$ gcc -o transformer transformer.c
```
This will create an executable file called `transformer` in the current directory. We can then run the program by typing its name and any arguments that we want to pass to it:
```
$ ./transformer input_file output_file;rules
```
For example, if we want to transform a file named `input_file` using the rules specified in `rules`, and save the result in a file named `output_file`, we can run the program as follows:
```
$ ./transformer input_file output_file;uppercase;lowercase;rotate;reverse
```
This will apply all of the transformation rules to the contents of `input_file` and save the result to `output_file`. The output file should have a transformed version of the contents of `input_file`, with the characters in uppercase, then converted to lowercase, rotated by 13 positions, and finally reversed.']

