Here is a possible implementation of the distributed processing pipeline using Node.js:
```
const { promisify } = require('util');
const { setTimeout } = require('timers').promise;

class Pipeline {
  constructor() {
    this.stages = [];
  }

  addStage(stage) {
    this.stages.push(stage);
  }

  async process(data) {
    for (const stage of this.stages) {
      try {
        data = await stage(data);
      } catch (error) {
        console.error('Stage failed:', error);
        return; // Fail the entire pipeline
      }
    }

    return data;
  }
}

class ReduceStage {
  constructor({ timeout }) {
    this.timeout = timeout;
  }

  async reduce(data) {
    const startTime = Date.now();

    // Perform the reduction operation asynchronously
    const result = await Promise.all(data);

    // Check if the stage timed out
    if (Date.now() - startTime >= this.timeout) {
      throw new Error(`Stage timed out after ${this.timeout}ms`);
    }

    return result;
  }
}
```
To use the pipeline, developers can create a `Pipeline` instance and add `ReduceStage` instances to it. Each `ReduceStage` has a timeout value that can be set in the constructor. The `process()` method of the `Pipeline` class takes an array of data as input and processes it through each stage in sequence. If any stage fails, the entire pipeline will fail gracefully and downstream stages will not be executed.
```
const pipeline = new Pipeline();
pipeline.addStage(new ReduceStage({ timeout: 1000 })); // Set a timeout of 1 second for the first stage
pipeline.addStage(new ReduceStage({ timeout: 5000 })); // Set a timeout of 5 seconds for the second stage

const data = [1, 2, 3];
const result = await pipeline.process(data);
console.log(result); // Outputs the reduced value of the array
```
In this implementation, each `ReduceStage` instance performs a reduction operation asynchronously and checks if it timed out. If a stage times out, an error is thrown and the entire pipeline fails gracefully. The `process()` method of the `Pipeline` class handles these errors and does not crash.

Testing the pipeline with various datasets, edge cases such as very large arrays, very short timeouts, and partial failures in external data sources can be done by creating test cases that exercise different scenarios and checking for expected behavior. For example, a test case for a very large array could check if the pipeline handles it efficiently without crashing, while a test case for a short timeout could check if the pipeline fails gracefully when a stage times out.
```
const data = [1, 2, 3]; // A small dataset
pipeline.process(data);

const largeData = []; // A large dataset
for (let i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
  largeData.push(Math.random());
}
pipeline.process(largeData);

const timeoutData = [1, 2, 3]; // A dataset with a short timeout
const reduceStage = new ReduceStage({ timeout: 50 });
pipeline.addStage(reduceStage);
pipeline.process(timeoutData);
```
By using the `Promise` API and the `timers` module in Node.js, this implementation ensures that the pipeline handles asynchronous operations efficiently and provides meaningful error messages for failed stages while maintaining user-friendly and scalable design principles."]

