To balance efficiency, usability, maintainability, and scalability, we use efficient data structures and algorithms to manage memory allocation and deallocation. We also consider potential memory fragmentation and cache performance when allocating memory for the three arrays. We also ensure that the solution scales well with increasing `range` values by using dynamic memory allocation and not hardcoding any fixed sizes.

In terms of engineering tradeoffs, we prioritize maintainability over raw speed, as we want to ensure that the code is easy to read and understand for future developers who may need to modify it. We also consider compatibility with existing logging standards by integrating with an external logging library. Finally, we prioritize thread-safety over performance, as we know that multi-threaded environments can be challenging to manage memory allocation and deallocation in a race-free manner.']

