This code creates a pool of LTE UE context release commands and uses it to allocate and deallocate new messages. It also adds and removes P-TMSI values from the pool as needed. The `validate_p_tmsi` function checks if the P-TMSI value is valid, and the `add_p_tmsi_to_pool` and `remove_p_tmsi_from_pool` functions add or remove a P-TMSI value from the pool as needed. The `process_messages` function loops through each message in the list, validates the P-TMSI value, adds the P-TMSI value to the pool if necessary, allocates a new message from the pool or uses an existing one, copies the P-TMSI value and NAS message to the new message, and adds the new message to the pool. Finally, it deallocates any free messages in the pool before returning.

### Example 2: Using a stack to manage memory allocation

In this example, we'll use a stack data structure to manage memory allocation for our program. We'll create a `Stack` class that has methods for pushing and popping values onto and off of the stack. We'll also create a `MemoryAllocator` class that has methods for allocating and deallocating memory on the stack.
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <stack>

using namespace std;

class MemoryAllocator {
public:
    MemoryAllocator(int size) {
        // allocate a block of memory on the stack
        mem = new char[size];
        top = 0;
    }

    ~MemoryAllocator() {
        delete[] mem;
    }

    void* allocate(int size) {
        if (top + size > MAX_SIZE) {
            throw "out of memory";
        }
        char* result = &mem[top];
        top += size;
        return result;
    }

    void deallocate() {
        top -= size;
    }

private:
    const int MAX_SIZE = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB
    char* mem;
    int top;
};

class Stack {
public:
    void push(int value) {
        MemoryAllocator& allocator = MemoryAllocator::getInstance();
        void* address = allocator.allocate(sizeof(value));
        *((int*)address) = value;
    }

    int pop() {
        MemoryAllocator& allocator = MemoryAllocator::getInstance();
        void* address = allocator.getLastAddress();
        int result = *((int*)address);
        allocator.deallocate();
        return result;
    }

private:
    static MemoryAllocator instance;
};
```
This code defines a `Stack` class that has methods for pushing and popping values onto and off of the stack. The `MemoryAllocator` class is used to manage memory allocation on the stack, and it has methods for allocating and deallocating memory. The `getInstance` method returns a reference to an instance of the `MemoryAllocator` class that is stored as a static variable in the `Stack` class. This allows the `MemoryAllocator` class to be instantiated only once, which can improve performance by reducing overhead associated with creating and destroying objects.

### Example 3: Using a linked list to store values

In this example, we'll use a linked list data structure to store values in our program. We'll create a `LinkedList` class that has methods for inserting and removing values from the list.
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

using namespace std;

class LinkedList {
public:
    void insert(int value) {
        Node* node = new Node();
        node->value = value;
        node->next = head;
        head = node;
    }

    int remove() {
        if (!head) {
            throw "empty list";
        }
        int result = head->value;
        Node* temp = head;
        head = head->next;
        delete temp;
        return result;
    }

private:
    struct Node {
        int value;
        Node* next;
    };

    unique_ptr<Node> head;
};
```
This code defines a `LinkedList` class that has methods for inserting and removing values from the list. The `insert` method creates a new node with the given value, sets its `next` field to point to the current head of the list, and then sets the head of the list to point to the new node. The `remove` method retrieves the value from the head of the list, sets the next field of the previous node to point to the next node in the list (if there is one), and then deletes the first node in the list.

### Example 4: Using a queue to store values

In this example, we'll use a queue data structure to store values in our program. We'll create a `Queue` class that has methods for inserting and removing values from the queue.
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

using namespace std;

class Queue {
public:
    void enqueue(int value) {
        Node* node = new Node();
        node->value = value;
        if (!head) {
            head = node;
        } else {
            tail->next = node;
        }
        tail = node;
    }

    int dequeue() {
        if (!head) {
            throw "empty queue";
        }
        int result = head->value;
        Node* temp = head;
        head = head->next;
        delete temp;
        return result;
    }

private:
    struct Node {
        int value;
        Node* next;
    };

    unique_ptr<Node> head;
};
```
