The Java method to establish and maintain connections to multiple remote addresses using NIO Channels is as follows:
```java
import java.nio.channels.Selector;
import java.nio.channels.SocketChannel;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Set;
import java.io.IOException;

public class MultiConnection {
    private int numConnections = 0;
    private Selector selector;
    private Set<SocketChannel> connections;

    public void init(int numConnections) throws IOException {
        this.numConnections = numConnections;
        selector = Selector.open();
        connections = new HashSet<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numConnections; i++) {
            SocketChannel channel = SocketChannel.open(new InetSocketAddress("localhost", 80));
            if (!channel.isConnected()) {
                throw new IOException("Failed to connect");
            }
            connections.add(channel);
        }
    }

    public void handleConnection() throws IOException {
        Iterator<SelectionKey> it = selector.selectedKeys().iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            SelectionKey key = it.next();
            if (key.isValid() && key.isConnectable()) {
                SocketChannel channel = (SocketChannel) key.channel();
                channel.finishConnect();
                connections.add(channel);
                System.out.println("Connected to " + channel.getRemoteAddress());
            }
        }
    }

    public void close() throws IOException {
        selector.close();
        for (SocketChannel channel : connections) {
            channel.close();
        }
    }
}
```
The above code establishes and maintains connections to multiple remote addresses using NIO Channels, where each connection is represented by a `SocketChannel`. The `init` method initializes the number of connections and opens a selector to monitor readiness events on all channels. The `handleConnection` method iterates over selected keys and checks for connectability events. If a channel is connected, it adds the channel to the set of active connections and prints a message indicating that the connection has been established. Finally, the `close` method closes the selector and all open connections.

Note: The above code is just an example and may need to be modified to fit your specific use case.']

