The function returns a pointer to the first element of the initialized array, or `NULL` if initialization fails. The `destroyNetworkConfig` function is used to deallocate resources associated with a `NetworkInterfaceConfig` structure. It frees the memory allocated for the neighbor cell list and then frees the `NetworkInterfaceConfig` structure itself.

This implementation meets the requirements of the problem, handling up to 1000 network interfaces and 100 neighbor cells per interface without significant performance degradation. It also provides a flexible configuration mechanism allowing users to specify custom SRS templates or override default neighbor cell list sizes for individual interfaces. Robust error handling is implemented using logging mechanisms that are non-intrusive and scalable."]

