# 4. Implementation

## 4.1. Functionality

The `LinkedList` class has the following methods:

* `add(int data)`: Adds a new node to the end of the list with the given `data`.
* `remove(int index)`: Removes and returns the node at the specified `index`. If `index` is out of bounds, it returns null.
* `indexOf(int data)`: Returns the index of the first occurrence of the specified `data`, or -1 if not found.
* `contains(int data)`: Returns true if the list contains the specified `data`, and false otherwise.
* `print()`: Prints all the nodes in the list, starting from the head.
* `clear()`: Removes all the nodes in the list.

## 4.2. Design

The `LinkedList` class is a generic class that can store any type of data. It uses a `Node` inner class to represent each node in the list, which contains a reference to the next node and some data. The `head`, `tail`, and `size` variables are used to keep track of the beginning, end, and number of nodes in the list respectively.

The methods `add(int data)`, `remove(int index)`, `indexOf(int data)`, `contains(int data)`, `print()`, and `clear()` are implemented using the following algorithms:

* Adding a new node at the end of the list:
	1. Create a new node with the given `data`.
	2. Set its next pointer to null.
	3. If the head is null, set it to the new node. Otherwise, set the tail's next pointer to the new node.
	4. Increment the size variable.
* Removing a node at the specified index:
	1. Check if the `index` is out of bounds and return null if so.
	2. If the `index` is 0, set the head to the next node and decrement the size variable.
	3. Otherwise, iterate through the list until reaching the previous node at the specified `index`.
	4. Set the previous node's next pointer to the next node after the one being removed.
	5. Free the memory used by the node being removed.
* Finding the index of a data:
	1. Iterate through the list until reaching the first occurrence of the specified `data`. If not found, return -1.
* Checking if a data is in the list:
	1. Iterate through the list until reaching the end. Return true if the `data` is found, and false otherwise.
* Printing all the nodes:
	1. Start from the head and iterate through each node while printing its data.
* Clearing the list:
	1. Set the head and tail to null.
	2. Iterate through each node until reaching the end and free the memory used by each one.

## 4.3. Code

Here is the code for the `LinkedList` class in Java:
```java
public class LinkedList<T> {
    private Node<T> head;
    private Node<T> tail;
    private int size;

    public void add(int data) {
        Node<T> newNode = new Node<>(data);
        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
        } else {
            tail.next = newNode;
        }
        tail = newNode;
        size++;
    }

    public int remove(int index) {
        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {
            return null;
        }
        Node<T> current = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {
            current = current.next;
        }
        int data = current.data;
        if (index == 0) {
            head = head.next;
        } else {
            Node<T> previous = getNode(index - 1);
            previous.next = current.next;
        }
        size--;
        return data;
    }

    public int indexOf(int data) {
        Node<T> current = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            if (current.data == data) {
                return i;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public boolean contains(int data) {
        Node<T> current = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            if (current.data == data) {
                return true;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public void print() {
        Node<T> current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.print(current.data + " ");
            current = current.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public void clear() {
        head = null;
        tail = null;
        size = 0;
    }

    private Node<T> getNode(int index) {
        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid index");
        }
        Node<T> current = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {
            current = current.next;
        }
        return current;
    }
}

class Node<T> {
    T data;
    Node<T> next;

    public Node(T data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```
